# A short story for the Aside engine.
#
# ELEVENTH REGISTER, AND THE FIRST THAT OFFERS THE SAME DECISION MORE THAN ONCE. Every story before this one
# moves forward: a choice leads to a scene, the scene leads to the next thing, and the reader is always further
# along than they were. This one circles. The ferryman is asked the same question at the same waterline on three
# different nights, and the only thing that changes between them is how many times he has said not yet - so the
# flags are the whole engine of the story, and the three endings are what a crossing, two deferrals, and a
# refusal respectively add up to.
#
# It is also the first story here about a job that is ENDING rather than one being done badly. The bridge opens
# on Monday. Nothing is broken, nobody is cruel, and the work was good for nineteen years.
#
# THE ART IS NOT DRAWN YET, AND THAT IS STATED RATHER THAN HIDDEN. The four backgrounds this story stages itself
# on - river_jetty, far_bank, dawn_river, dawn_bridge - have no files under art/stories/the-last-crossing/, so
# WebExport reports "no art for: ..." for every one of them and the phone build carries the words only. That is a
# known state in this library and not a fault in the script: WebExport's own header names two other stories in it.
# The names are reserved so the art can be dropped in without touching the script, and drawing it is the
# remaining work - the README's "each has its own art" is not yet true of this one.

title: The Last Crossing
author: Roxanne
start: jetty

== jetty ==
bg river_jetty
music sea_swell
The jetty is four planks and a bell, and the bell is older than the planks.
You have poled this crossing for nineteen years. The river is not wide. It has never been wide.

Upstream there is a line of dark with a light at each end, and a crane above it that has not moved all week.

The bell goes. It is nearly midnight.

* Answer it. -> passenger
* Let it ring itself out. -> quiet
* Look upstream first. -> upstream

== upstream ==
bg river_jetty
They are painting the railings. You can see that much even in the dark, because they have left a lamp on.
Painting the railings is the last thing anybody does to a bridge.
~ looked true
-> jetty_after

== jetty_after ==
The bell has stopped, which is what it does if you leave it alone, and then it is just a bell
hanging over water doing nothing at all.
-> jetty_two

== quiet ==
bg river_jetty
You let it ring. Three times, then a pause, then nothing.
Whoever it was has walked back up the lane, and you did not see their face, and you will not think about
that until later, which is a thing you know about yourself.
~ refused_once true
-> jetty_two

== jetty_two ==
The water goes past the way it always goes past, and you stand on your own jetty with the pole in your hand
like a man holding a stick he has forgotten the reason for.

* Go home. It is nearly midnight and the bridge opens Monday. -> go_home
* Take the pole out of its rest and put it back. Twice. -> fidgit [if looked]
* Answer it, if it goes again. -> passenger_two [if refused_once]

== fidgit ==
bg river_jetty
You take the pole out of its rest and put it back, and then do it again, because the rest is worn
and the pole has a place it likes, and after nineteen years a man is allowed a superstition
or two about a piece of wood.
-> jetty_two

== go_home ==
bg river_jetty
You go home. The jetty stays where it is, which is the arrangement you have had for nineteen years
and which ends on Monday whether anyone winds it up or not.
-> dawn_bridge

== passenger ==
bg river_jetty
sfx bell
A woman with a holdall and a coat that is not a coat for this weather.

ferry: It is midnight.
passenger: I know what time it is.
passenger: I need the other side.
ferry: There is a bridge. It opens Monday.
passenger: It is not Monday.

She is right about that, which is the difficulty with most people who come down the lane at this hour.

* Pole her across. -> cross_now
* Ask her what is on the other side. -> ask
* Tell her to come back Monday. -> defer

== ask ==
bg river_jetty
passenger: My sister. She is not well and she is not going to get better, and the bus
passenger: does not run the lane at night.
passenger: You are the last person on this river who can do anything about that.
~ asked true
-> passenger_choice

== passenger_choice ==
The holdall is heavy. She has not put it down, which tells you she expects to be told no.

* Pole her across. -> cross_now
* Tell her to come back Monday. -> defer

== cross_now ==
bg far_bank
sfx footstep
You pole her across. It takes four minutes, which it has taken four minutes for nineteen years.
She steps off onto the far stones and does not thank you, and then does, and then goes,
and the last thing you hear is the holdall knocking against the lane wall going up.

You pole back. The river is exactly as wide going back.
-> far_bank

== far_bank ==
bg far_bank
music sea_swell
Nineteen years, and the thing you will remember is not the crossing.
It is that she did not put the holdall down until she was on the far stones,
which is what being braced looks like when nobody has been kind to you for a while.
-> END

== defer ==
bg river_jetty
ferry: Come back Monday. The bridge will be open and it will cost you nothing.
passenger: And tonight it costs you four minutes.

She goes back up the lane. You listen to the holdall the whole way, which is a long way.
~ deferred_once true
-> night_two

== night_two ==
bg river_jetty
music sea_swell
The next night. Same bell, same water, and you are early because you told yourself you would be.

The bell goes at half eleven, which is earlier than last night, which means she has been walking
around the lane for an hour deciding whether to come back.

* Answer it. -> passenger_two
* Sit down on the jetty and wait for the bell to stop. -> wait_two

== wait_two ==
bg river_jetty
You sit on the planks. The bell goes three times and stops, and then goes once more, which is either
her making sure or her giving up, and you will never know which, and that is the part you keep.
~ deferred_twice true
-> night_three

== passenger_two ==
bg river_jetty
ferry: It is midnight again.
passenger: It was always going to be midnight.
passenger: Are you poling or are you standing?

* Pole her across. -> cross_late
* Say not tonight. -> not_tonight

== not_tonight ==
bg river_jetty
ferry: Not tonight.
passenger: Monday, then. And you will be on the jetty watching the bridge, and I will be on it,
passenger: and neither of us will say anything about tonight, because there will not be a tonight.
~ deferred_twice true
-> night_three

== cross_late ==
bg far_bank
sfx footstep
You pole her across. It takes four minutes and then the second four minutes coming back,
and you are in bed by one, and nobody will ever know, and that is what makes it a job
rather than a favour.
~ crossed true
-> dawn_river

== night_three ==
bg river_jetty
music sea_swell
Sunday. The last night the jetty is anything.

She is already on the jetty when you get there, sitting on the planks with the holdall on her knees
and a ticket in her coat pocket that says Monday, because the bridge does not need you to be brave,
it only needs you to be patient.

ferry: You have a ticket.
passenger: It was cheaper than the fare a person would have charged me.
passenger: I came down to give you the last night.

* Pole her across tonight. -> cross_final
* Sit down beside her and do not pole anybody anywhere. -> sit_final
* Tell her you heard the bell. Both times. -> confess [if !deferred_once]
* Tell her you stood on the planks and let it ring. -> confess [if deferred_twice]

== confess ==
bg river_jetty
ferry: I heard it. I stood on these planks and let it ring.
passenger: I know. I watched you not answer it.
passenger: That is why I got the ticket.
~ confessed true
-> night_three_choice

== night_three_choice ==
The water goes past. She has put the holdall down on the planks, which is new.

* Pole her across. -> cross_final
* Sit down beside her. -> sit_final
* Ask her what she told her sister. -> sister [if asked]

== sister ==
bg river_jetty
passenger: I told her I had a way of getting there.
passenger: I did not tell her that it was a man on a jetty at midnight who keeps saying not yet.
passenger: She would have asked me what he was like, and I would have had to say kind,
passenger: and then she would have wanted to meet you, and you are not somebody anybody meets.
-> night_three_choice

== sit_final ==
bg river_jetty
You sit down. The planks are cold and the river goes past and neither of you says much,
and after a while she says the thing that you will end up telling whoever asks you about it.

passenger: It was never the crossing.
passenger: It was that there was a person down here at midnight with a pole.
-> dawn_river

== cross_final ==
bg far_bank
sfx footstep
You pole her across on the last night. It takes four minutes. It takes four minutes every time.
She steps off onto the far stones and puts the holdall down and turns round, which is a thing
she has not done any of the other nights.
passenger: Thank you. I would have understood if you had not.
ferry: I know.
~ crossed true
-> dawn_river

== dawn_river ==
bg dawn_river
music sea_swell
The morning comes the way mornings do: without ceremony and slightly early, and the crane has moved.

The bridge opens at nine. It is a good bridge. It will do the job for sixty years
and nobody will ever be down here at midnight because of it.

You pole across once more with nothing to carry, because the jetty is yours until Monday
and a man is allowed to finish the thing he started.

* Say it out loud. -> last_word [if confessed]
* Say it out loud anyway. -> last_word [if !confessed]
* Think about the four minutes. -> four_minutes [if crossed]
* Let the water say it. -> END

== last_word ==
ferry: That is the last crossing.
ferry: Somebody should say it out loud.
-> END

== four_minutes ==
bg dawn_river
Four minutes each way. It has taken four minutes for nineteen years and it will take four minutes
exactly once more, with nothing to carry.

Nobody will ever ask you what you did on the last night the jetty was anything.
You will answer it anyway, in your own head, for years: I poled her across.

ferry: That is the last crossing.
-> END

== dawn_bridge ==
bg dawn_bridge
sfx bell
The morning comes the way mornings do: without ceremony and slightly early.

The bridge opens at nine, and people walk across it the way people walk across new things,
which is quickly, and looking at their feet.

You did not pole anybody anywhere on any of the last three nights. Nobody will ever know that either.
There is a bell on the jetty and it still rings, and it will ring for a while, and then it will be
an old bell over water that nobody hears.

ferry: Monday.

-> END
